ZTEXT00100001ec284414e780000000
Zero source syntax - data first

Zero has its own source grammar. A statement starts with the value being
bound, a control marker, or an explicit effect. Variable references carry @;
expression operations carry :; side effects carry !. Periods end statements,
square brackets delimit blocks and arguments, and | separates arguments.

Bindings
    10 => @count.
    @count + 1 -> @count.
    "Ready\n" => @message.

=> declares a new top-level variable. -> assigns an existing variable.
The expression is evaluated first. The new name becomes visible only after
its initializer; duplicate names and self-reference in an initializer fail.
Declarations inside control blocks are rejected. Variables persist for the
whole top-level program; there are no implicit globals or shadowed names.

Variable references always use @name. Names are ASCII, case-sensitive,
1..31 characters: a letter or underscore, followed by letters/digits/_ .
The @ prefix does not count toward that length. Operation names from every
target and retired language keywords are reserved and cannot be variable
names. A name does not become available merely because its operation is
unsupported by the selected target.

Control flow
    ? @count == 10 [
        !put[65].
    ] ~ [
        !put[66].
    ]

    ?? @count < 20 [
        @count + 1 -> @count.
    ]

? selects a block when its condition is nonzero. The optional ~ block runs
when the condition is zero. ?? repeats a block while its condition is nonzero.
Control blocks do not take a trailing period. Whitespace and indentation do
not determine structure. # starts a comment through the end of the line.

Operations and effects
    :reserve[64] => @buffer.
    :load8[@buffer] => @byte.
    !store8[@buffer | 65].
    :write[@buffer | 1] => @written.
    :bitxor[0xff00 | 0x0ff0] => @bits.
    :bitand[@bits | 255] -> @bits.
    :shr[@bits | 4] -> @bits.
    :arg0[] => @boot.
    !halt.

: introduces a value-producing operation. It always has argument brackets,
including [] for a zero-argument operation such as arg0. ! introduces an
effect statement. Effects with arguments use brackets; effects without
arguments are written !halt. / !disable. / !enable. where the target allows
them. Arguments are evaluated from left to right. | is only an argument
separator, not a bitwise operator. Only the operations supplied by a target
are available; target restrictions and hardware ABI contracts still apply.

Hosted file-descriptor operations retain exactly the same grammar:
    :pread[3 | @buffer | 512 | 4096] => @read_count.
    :pwrite[3 | @buffer | @read_count | 8192] => @write_count.
    :fsync[3] => @flush_status.
The invoking host supplies descriptor3 in this example. Return values must be
checked for short I/O and negative errno represented as unsigned 64-bit values.

Examples for firmware targets
    :cpuid[0x80000001 | 3] => @features.
    :memmap[0 | 0x6600] => @next_record.
    :biosmove[0x6800 | 16384] => @copy_status.
    !out8[0x3f8 | 65].
    !out32[0xcf8 | 0x80000000].
    :in32[0xcfc] => @pci_identity.
    :call[@function | @a1 | @a2 | @a3 | @a4 | @a5] -> @status.
    !longjump[8 | 0x20000 | @handoff | 0x90000].
    !protectedjump[8 | 0x20000 | @handoff | 0x90000].

biosmove is a REAL-only INT15/AH87 block transfer using a caller-initialized
48-byte descriptor table; see portability/guest/biosmove/README.ztext.
The last two are real-mode architectural transfer primitives. The Zero
loader supplies the GDT, memory layout and control-register policy first.
protectedjump requires a flat 32-bit code descriptor at the supplied selector and
a flat writable data descriptor at selector 0x10. It far-transfers to a 32-bit
ABI shim, loads DS/ES/SS/FS/GS=0x10, clears DF, and enters the supplied kernel
with cdecl arg0 on its new stack and arg1=0. A return address leads to a halt
loop. The stack is aligned so entry ESP modulo 16 is 12. It does not construct
tables, read disks, select CPU modes, or enable paging by itself.

Cooperative continuation forms
    :fork[@child_context | @child_stack | 81920] -> @branch.
    ? @branch == 1 [
        !switch[@child_context | @parent_context].
    ]
    !switch[@parent_context | @child_context].

This is a grammar example using already allocated, aligned context and stack
memory. fork returns 0 in the parent; its suspended child receives 1 when
first switched to. switch is an effect that later resumes at its continuation.
The source controls scheduling. Independent stacks do not imply separate
address spaces: pre-existing pointers remain shared. See LANGUAGE.ztext for
memory ownership, minimum stack sizes, target restrictions and native ABI.

Expressions
Numbers are decimal or 0x hexadecimal; strings use double quotes. Strings
retain the ASCII escapes \n, \r, \t, \\, and \". Arithmetic parentheses are
available for grouping; they are not operation-call delimiters.
Precedence: primary/grouping, unary -, *, +/-, then one unsigned comparison
(== != < <= > >=). Comparisons produce 0 or 1; arithmetic wraps at the target
value width. Existing x86-64 targets use u64; the real-mode subset uses u32.
The protected-mode IA32 target also preserves full-u64 values; X86.ztext
documents its paired-register representation and firmware ABI.

There are no implicit assignments or conversions between operation forms.
A value-producing operation cannot be used as an effect merely by changing
its prefix. Bind its result to a variable when the operation returns a value.

Migration boundary
The old declaration-first statements, bare variable references, alphabetic
control keywords, braces, semicolon endings and parenthesized operation calls
are not accepted aliases. Source files must use this grammar consistently.
The compiler is itself written in this grammar. Self-hosting does not add
assembly blocks, imported compiler code or a second source language.
See LANGUAGE.ztext, REAL.ztext and X86.ztext for target
limits and operations, and verification reports for the tested scope.

Shared grammar across output targets
The syntax above is Zero syntax. The same compiler can target Linux ELF
(default/--host), Windows x64 PE (--windows), native Zero OS applications
(--app64/--app32), raw kernels (--raw/--raw32), UEFI (--efi/--efi32), or the
real-mode BIOS subset (--real). Target flags change entry/output contracts
and allowed operations, not statement spelling. See LANGUAGE.ztext.
Ezero has its own higher-level source grammar, documented in ezero/README.ztext
and ezero/ABI.ztext; unquoted UTF-8 phrases and one-based collections belong to
Ezero and are not silently accepted as aliases by the Zero parser.

Native host operation examples (same Zero grammar)
    :host[] => @platform.
    :argcount[] => @count.
    :argument[1] => @path.
    :fileopen[@path | 1] => @handle.
    :filewrite[@handle | "Hello" | 5] => @written.
    :filesync[@handle] => @flushed.
    :fileclose[@handle] => @closed.
    :hostwait[250] => @waited.
    :program[] => @executable_path.
    :hostalloc[4096] => @pages.
    :hostprotect[@pages | 4096 | 1] => @protected.
    :hostfree[@pages | 4096] => @released.

These are form examples; real programs must validate arguments and check every
return before using a handle or pointer. Mutation primitives still use ':'
because they return status; !fileclose and other effect aliases are rejected.
filesync requests a file flush; it does not make directory renames durable.
hostwait accepts milliseconds0..86400000 and suspends the native thread;
larger unsigned values fail immediately. Both return0 or unsigned-1.
fileopen modes 0..4 are explicit low-level API constants, while user argument
indices start at 1. Guest builds return unavailable-service sentinels described
in LANGUAGE.ztext. Ezero offers separate high-level host()/arguments()/readfile()/
writefile()/fileexists() forms; those are not Zero grammar aliases.
